Make FCOORD's default constructor constexpr-usable on GCC 8.

Tesseract 5.5.3 marked ~24 of the ICOORD/FCOORD free operators `constexpr`
(5.5.0 had none). Several of the FCOORD ones declare a local and assign to it:

    constexpr inline FCOORD operator!(const FCOORD &src) {
      FCOORD result;                  // <-- FCOORD() = default, members untouched
      result.xcoord = -src.ycoord;
      ...
    }

Before C++20 (P1331R2, implemented in GCC 10), a constexpr function may not
contain a variable of non-trivial-for-constexpr type left uninitialized, so GCC 8
hard-errors with "uninitialized variable 'result' in 'constexpr' function" at
nine sites: points.h:184, 187, 306, 519, 535, 551, 581, 635, 646. GCC 9 accepts
it as an extension, which is why this is invisible on every other target's
toolchain -- the bookeen TC is the only GCC 8 in the tree.

ICOORD, immediately above in the same header, already does the right thing:

    constexpr ICOORD() : xcoord(0), ycoord(0) {}

so its identical operators compile cleanly. This makes FCOORD match that idiom
rather than adding a -std= override or disabling the diagnostic; zero-init of two
floats in the default ctor is what ICOORD already pays, and it also fixes the
class's uninitialized-read footgun.

The second hunk makes the (float,float) ctor use a member init list, so it is
usable in constant expressions too and no longer relies on assignment into
default-initialized members.

--- i/src/ccstruct/points.h
+++ w/src/ccstruct/points.h
@@ -184,14 +184,11 @@
 class TESS_API FCOORD {
 public:
   /// empty constructor
-  FCOORD() = default;
+  constexpr FCOORD() : xcoord(0.0f), ycoord(0.0f) {}
   /// constructor
   ///@param xvalue x value
   ///@param yvalue y value
-  FCOORD(float xvalue, float yvalue) {
-    xcoord = xvalue; // set coords
-    ycoord = yvalue;
-  }
+  constexpr FCOORD(float xvalue, float yvalue) : xcoord(xvalue), ycoord(yvalue) {}
   constexpr FCOORD(              // make from ICOORD
       ICOORD icoord) : xcoord(icoord.xcoord), ycoord(icoord.ycoord) {}

